<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen</h1><p class="st-meta">1576 CE – 1850 CE · capital Sigmaringen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Catholic Swabian branch of the Hohenzollerns, ruling from Sigmaringen castle on the Danube, which later gave Romania its kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When Count Charles I of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern</a> died in 1576, his lands were divided among his sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-count-of-hohenzollern-sigmaringen">Charles II</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> and Veringen on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, founding the Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen line, and the counts were raised to princes in 1623.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The princes ruled a small Catholic territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen's</a> castle on a rock above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, under strong <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> influence since parts of their lands were Austrian fiefs. Thanks partly to Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalie-zephyrine-of-salm-kyrburg">Amalie Zephyrine</a>, a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais">Joséphine de Beauharnais</a>, the principality survived the mediatisation of 1806, joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and even gained territory.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the revolution of 1848, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-anthony-prince-of-hohenzollern">Charles Anthony</a> ceded his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to his Prussian cousins in 1849, and in 1850 the principality became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol</a> became prince and later king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>, and another son's candidacy for the Spanish throne helped trigger the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-hohenzollern-sigmaringen.jpg" alt="Principality of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vichycombo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hohenzollern_(1576%E2%80%931850).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1576 CE – 1850 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sigmaringen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles II, Anton Aloys, Charles Anthony</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1713&amp;focus=principality-of-hohenzollern-sigmaringen" aria-label="Principality of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4894 495 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5214 751L5208 743M5214 751L5229 694M5214 751L5199 691M5214 751L5199 691M5214 751L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5208 743h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sigmaringen. <a href="/atlas?year=1713&amp;focus=principality-of-hohenzollern-sigmaringen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
